<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naqsh-e Rostam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naqsh-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> is an ancient necropolis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It contains the rock-cut tombs of Achaemenid kings and later Sasanian rock reliefs. Among these is the relief celebrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>'s triumph over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, including the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Emperor Valerian</a> in 260 CE, a key monument of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam.jpg" alt="Naqsh-e Rostam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maasaak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naqsh-e_Rustam_necropolis_in_Iran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
